    "state_reason": null,
    "title": "contrib: detached-sig-create.sh passes the signing passphrases as arguments",
    "body": "`contrib/macdeploy/detached-sig-create.sh` reads two passphrases and passes them to `signapple` with `--passphrase`. Every `sign` call gets the code signing key passphrase. Every `notarize` call gets the App Store Connect key passphrase. While a call runs, its passphrase is in the process arguments, which `ps` shows. `contrib/windeploy/detached-sig-create.sh` passes its key passphrase to `osslsigncode sign` with `-pass`. Both came in with #31407.\n\nOn Windows, the script could pipe the passphrase to `osslsigncode sign -readpass -`, which reads it from stdin. osslsigncode 2.14, the version Guix pins, has `-readpass`. On macOS, `signapple` takes the passphrase only from `--passphrase` or from a prompt (3fab3bb, its master and the Guix pin). Without `--passphrase` it would prompt once per call. So `signapple` would first need an option that reads the passphrase from a file descriptor, a file or the environment.\n\nI ran the macOS script with a stub `signapple` that prints its own `ps` line, and each passphrase appeared there. I did not run either script with the real tools or keys. Would the codesigners take a change along these lines?\n\nI found this during a large audit of Bitcoin Core that I did with the help of LLM tools. I understand the scripts and will answer questions myself.\n",
